Continuation of ai/eval/ (docs/evidence/16-ai-eval-corpus.log): the grading
harness itself, per PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §6.4's requirement that the
evaluation mechanism exist and be trustworthy before any model is scored
with it - "A grader nobody has tried to fool with a bad answer is not yet a
grader," proven here by actually trying.

Added:
- ai/eval/schema/ - the Case/Expected shape, shared by the generator
  (ai/eval/gen) and the harness so they cannot silently drift onto two
  different definitions of the same case (this was a real risk: they
  started as two separately-typed copies during development and were
  unified once the harness needed the same shape the generator produces).
- ai/eval/harness/harness.go - Grade(caseID, expected, knownEventIDs, out)
  checks a model's constrained-JSON answer (ModelOutput, matching §6.2's
  required shape exactly: summary, ranked_hypotheses[], evidence_event_ids[],
  counter_evidence[], unknowns[], confidence_ceiling, next_checks[]) against
  one case's expectations. Hard violations (fabricated citations, exceeding
  the evidence's own confidence ceiling, a confident answer where refusal
  was correct, no unknowns named on a refusal) are separated from scored-not-
  blocking metrics (Top-1/Top-3 cause hit, citation precision) - matching
  §6.4's own split between release-blocking criteria and per-candidate
  tracked numbers.

9 unit tests prove the harness actually catches what it should, not just
that it runs: a fabricated event-ID citation always fails regardless of
anything else; confidence above the evidence's own ceiling fails; offering
nothing on a real, findable cause fails; a confident hypothesis on a
refusal-expected case fails (the exact false-causality §6.2 forbids); a
refusal naming no unknowns fails (a bare refusal is not more useful than a
wrong answer); a correct, honest refusal passes; Top-3-but-not-Top-1 and
partial citation precision are scored correctly, not rounded to pass/fail.

TestHarnessRunsAgainstEveryGeneratedCase is the integration check: it loads
every one of the 33 real cases ai/eval/gen produced from corpus/v1/, builds
a synthetic "perfect" answer directly from each case's own Expected block,
and grades it. All 33 pass. This is not a test of any model (none exists
yet) - it is a test that the harness itself is internally consistent with
the corpus it grades, which has to be true before either is trusted.

Verification: gofmt/go vet clean; full repository test suite passes on both
Windows and Linux (the latter with -race) with these new packages included;
regenerating the corpus (`go run ./ai/eval/gen/`) after the schema
unification still produces the same 33 cases correctly.

Still not done: no actual model, no download, no benchmark run against a
real candidate - this remains exactly the "yardstick before the AI" the
plan requires, nothing past it.
